  "query_preview": "..High pressure aloft will remain centered west of the \nsouthern Baja Peninsula through Sunday. This will result in above \nnormal high temperatures across southeast Arizona through Monday, \nwith temperatures topping out 6-10 degrees above normal. High \ntemperatures will be in the lower to mid 70s across valley locations \nof Graham, Cochise and Santa Cruz counties, with highs in the mid \n70s to around 80 degrees across the lower elevations of Pinal and \nPima counties. With Arizona on the front side of the ridge and \nnorthwest flow aloft, high clouds will move into the area this \nmorning with periods of high clouds persisting through Monday.  \n\nThe ridge breaks down on Monday, with near zonal flow across the \nregion.  A progressive Pacific storm system then moves across the \nregion during the middle of next week. There are some minor timing \ndifferences between the ECMWF/GFS, with the ECMWF the quicker of the \ntwo solutions. The ensembles and cluster means both favor this \nquicker solution. The current forecast has POPs increasing late in \nthe day Tuesday and especially Tuesday night, with high POPs \nareawide Wednesday before winding down from west to east late \nWednesday night into midday Thursday. The current forecasted storm \ntotal rainfall in the valleys is between 0.50-1.0 inches, with 1.0-\n1.5 inches in the mountains. Snow levels at this time look fairly \nhigh, generally above 7500, with total snowfall in the northern Sky \nIslands (Mt Lemmon and Mt Graham) of 5-8 inches and potentially up \nto 10 inches in the White Mountains of northern Greenlee County. \nHigh temperatures fall to 6-9 degrees below normal Wednesday and \nThursday of next week with the passage of this system. Highs will be \nin the upper 50s to lower 60s across valley locations in Graham, \nCochise and Santa Cruz counties, with highs in the lower to mid 60s \nacross the lower elevations of Pinal and Pima counties.\n\nBehind the storm system, weak shortwave ridging takes place late \nnext week into the first part of the weekend. High temperatures will \nwarm slowly, but still remain 3-5 degrees below normal.",
